(5791424, 840540, 86447, '<td valign="top">While I don''t find photographing a dead mouse in good condition nearly as disgusting as one decomposing on the bottom of a pool    *grin*  , I do appreciate Morgan''s truthfulness and the fact that MINE is the first image he''s commented on in over 4.5 years.  :)   Photography that brings out a strong reaction is good, I think.  <br/> <br/>Everyone has his ideas about what''s proper.  Some disagree with nudity, some with shooting animals kept in zoos... and so on.   Some even oppose black and white images.   To each his/her own, I say.   <br/> <br/>No animal was harmed in the making of my image... and so I say...<br/> <br/>Life is good, even when it''s not.<br/> <br/>~Lydia</td>', 'While I don''t find photographing a dead mouse in good condition nearly as disgusting as one decomposing on the bottom of a pool    *grin*  , I do appreciate Morgan''s truthfulness and the fact that MINE is the first image he''s commented on in over 4.5 years.  :)   Photography that brings out a strong reaction is good, I think.   Everyone has his ideas about what''s proper.  Some disagree with nudity, some with shooting animals kept in zoos... and so on.   Some even oppose black and white images.   To each his/her own, I say.    No animal was harmed in the making of my image... and so I say... Life is good, even when it''s not. ~Lydia', '2010-01-02 14:15:32.000000', NULL, 0),
(5791502, 841115, 88136, '<td valign="top">wow!! can i have the recipe?</td>', 'wow!! can i have the recipe?', '2010-01-02 15:27:43.000000', NULL, 0),
(5791564, 840540, 96530, '<td valign="top">Lydia Dear, I was hesitant to comment on this shot when I first saw it even though I found it rather repulsive. Now seeing the positive way some have reacted to this image as someone who feels compassion for all creatures great and small feel I should say something. I really don''t think you and some others here have taken the time to consider the ethics of what you have done here? This was once a living creature with a soul that probably died a pretty horrible death by drowning in your pond. It would be one thing to use the body to sustain that of another or even arguably for science. But to use it in this way for something as trivial as a ribbon at DPC I feel is really quite awful. And I cringe at the thought that some ribbon hounds here at DPC will see this entry and be inspired to dig up more dead stuff or capture other critters in a manner that causes them discomfort or even eventual death.<br/>So kudos for a shot very well captured Lydia, but I hope you and all other members here at DPC never cross this ethical boundary again. One that as a good person you just didn''t think about enough at the time you crossed it :-)<br/><br/><i>Message edited by author 2010-01-02 16:38:46.</i></td>', 'Lydia Dear, I was hesitant to comment on this shot when I first saw it even though I found it rather repulsive. Now seeing the positive way some have reacted to this image as someone who feels compassion for all creatures great and small feel I should say something. I really don''t think you and some others here have taken the time to consider the ethics of what you have done here? This was once a living creature with a soul that probably died a pretty horrible death by drowning in your pond. It would be one thing to use the body to sustain that of another or even arguably for science. But to use it in this way for something as trivial as a ribbon at DPC I feel is really quite awful. And I cringe at the thought that some ribbon hounds here at DPC will see this entry and be inspired to dig up more dead stuff or capture other critters in a manner that causes them discomfort or even eventual death.So kudos for a shot very well captured Lydia, but I hope you and all other members here at DPC never cross this ethical boundary again. One that as a good person you just didn''t think about enough at the time you crossed it :-)', '2010-01-02 16:03:10.000000', '2010-01-02 16:38:46.000000', 0),
(5791673, 840540, 54645, '<td valign="top">Let''s not beat Lydia up too much, value judgements should be left at the door. I think the ethical boundary mentioned is fuzzy, but appropriate, and open for interpretation for many photographic subjects.<br/> <br/>Have these crossed a line? <br/> <br/><a href="//www.dpchallenge.com/image.php?IMAGE_ID=650782" rel="nofollow" target="_blank"><img alt="'' . substr(''//images.dpchallenge.com/images_challenge/0-999/821/120/Copyrighted_Image_Reuse_Prohibited_650782.jpg'', strrpos(''//images.dpchallenge.com/images_challenge/0-999/821/120/Copyrighted_Image_Reuse_Prohibited_650782.jpg'', ''/'') + 1) . ''" border="0" src="//images.dpchallenge.com/images_challenge/0-999/821/120/Copyrighted_Image_Reuse_Prohibited_650782.jpg"/></a> Should be in water, correct?<br/><a href="//www.dpchallenge.com/image.php?IMAGE_ID=493258" rel="nofollow" target="_blank"><img alt="'' . substr(''//images.dpchallenge.com/images_challenge/0-999/661/120/Copyrighted_Image_Reuse_Prohibited_493258.jpg'', strrpos(''//images.dpchallenge.com/images_challenge/0-999/661/120/Copyrighted_Image_Reuse_Prohibited_493258.jpg'', ''/'') + 1) . ''" border="0" src="//images.dpchallenge.com/images_challenge/0-999/661/120/Copyrighted_Image_Reuse_Prohibited_493258.jpg"/></a> Randomly picked this image, but in general, are we exploiting the homeless?<br/><a href="//www.dpchallenge.com/image.php?IMAGE_ID=327615" rel="nofollow" target="_blank"><img alt="'' . substr(''//images.dpchallenge.com/images_challenge/0-999/489/120/Copyrighted_Image_Reuse_Prohibited_327615.jpg'', strrpos(''//images.dpchallenge.com/images_challenge/0-999/489/120/Copyrighted_Image_Reuse_Prohibited_327615.jpg'', ''/'') + 1) . ''" border="0" src="//images.dpchallenge.com/images_challenge/0-999/489/120/Copyrighted_Image_Reuse_Prohibited_327615.jpg"/></a> Discussed in the past, but a good example of how insect photography may be acheived. &lt;Sorry Jason! First image that came to mind!&gt;<br/> <br/>and, finally, my own exploitation of a dead critter. <br/> <br/><a href="//www.dpchallenge.com/image.php?IMAGE_ID=810029" rel="nofollow" target="_blank"><img alt="'' . substr(''//images.dpchallenge.com/images_challenge/1000-1999/1071/120/Copyrighted_Image_Reuse_Prohibited_810029.jpg'', strrpos(''//images.dpchallenge.com/images_challenge/1000-1999/1071/120/Copyrighted_Image_Reuse_Prohibited_810029.jpg'', ''/'') + 1) . ''" border="0" src="//images.dpchallenge.com/images_challenge/1000-1999/1071/120/Copyrighted_Image_Reuse_Prohibited_810029.jpg"/></a></td>', 'Let''s not beat Lydia up too much, value judgements should be left at the door. I think the ethical boundary mentioned is fuzzy, but appropriate, and open for interpretation for many photographic subjects. Have these crossed a line?   Should be in water, correct? Randomly picked this image, but in general, are we exploiting the homeless? Discussed in the past, but a good example of how insect photography may be acheived. <Sorry Jason! First image that came to mind!> and, finally, my own exploitation of a dead critter.  ', '2010-01-02 17:18:22.000000', NULL, 0),
